# `s/\(a\)\1/DOUBLE/` -- a back-reference, the construct furthest out of reach.
#
# A back-reference needs the engine to remember what a group matched, and there
# are no groups here to remember. Real sed answers `DOUBLE b`; this used to
# answer `aa b` -- no match, silently, because `\(`, `\)` and `\1` were three
# literals that happened not to be in the text.
#
# Kept beside the group case because it is the one that cannot be fixed by a
# smaller engine: a back-reference is what stops an implementation simulating
# the automaton, and it is the reason lib/re.sol's two dialects have to be
# designed together rather than one after the other.
args: 's/\(a\)\1/DOUBLE/'
input:
aa b
abab c
